    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Put the question to a room of contractors and the vote splits, usually loudly. The truth is less dramatic: the top drills and impact drivers from these two brands are so evenly matched that most people could not pick one from the other on a jobsite. The real differences sit elsewhere, in the battery systems, the warranty terms, the specialty tools each lineup offers and how each brand reaches the shelf. Decide on those, because after the third battery, changing sides gets expensive.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Milwaukee vs DeWalt: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Pick Milwaukee if</strong> you work in plumbing, electrical, HVAC or mechanical trades, want the deepest range of specialty tools on one battery, or value a five-year warranty on most M18 and M12 tools. <strong>Pick DeWalt if</strong> you frame, build or remodel, like shopping at Lowe's and other big retailers, want FLEXVOLT 60V saws, or chase kits bundled with free batteries. For a homeowner, either one is a sound choice for the next decade.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
