        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Owns Milwaukee and DeWalt?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee belongs to Techtronic Industries and DeWalt to Stanley Black and Decker, and each is the professional brand of its parent. Milwaukee Electric Tool started out in Wisconsin in the 1920s, and TTI, a company based in Hong Kong, has owned it since 2005. The same group owns or licenses Ryobi, AEG and Hart, along with floor care names such as Hoover. Milwaukee sits at the top of that family and has its own engineering organization, kept apart from Ryobi's.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt traces its roots to 1924. Its owner, the American firm Stanley Black and Decker, also holds Craftsman, Black and Decker, Porter-Cable, Stanley and Irwin. Within that group DeWalt plays the professional role, and, as with Milwaukee, its engineering runs separately from the consumer brands that share its parent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where are the tools built?</h3>

          <p>
            Both companies produce tools in a range of countries, China, Mexico, Vietnam and the United States among them, with the location depending on the tool. Each also assembles some products in the US from parts sourced worldwide. Because the country of origin differs from model to model and can change over the years, read the box for the specific tool when it matters to you instead of assuming by brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do sister brands share batteries?</h3>

          <p>
            No, and that is the one place ownership actually affects a buyer. Ryobi and Milwaukee share TTI as a parent, and Craftsman and DeWalt share Stanley Black and Decker, yet none of those batteries cross over between brands unless you add a third-party adapter. A common owner does not mean a common platform.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is DeWalt 20V More Powerful Than Milwaukee M18?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. DeWalt's 20V figure is the maximum initial voltage with no load, while the nominal voltage is 18 volts, exactly what Milwaukee prints on M18. On paper neither platform has more power. What separates individual tools is the motor tier and electronics inside them, so it helps to know how each brand arranges its lineup.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Milwaukee splits M18, M12 and MX FUEL</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>M18</strong> is Milwaukee's core 18-volt system. It spans hundreds of tools for drilling, fastening, cutting, lighting, plumbing, electrical, automotive and outdoor work. <strong>M12</strong>, the compact 12-volt line, runs unusually deep: ratchets, PEX expanders, inspection cameras, small press tools and right-angle drills are all on it, and many tradespeople keep a few for close-quarters work. <strong>MX FUEL</strong> stands apart as a heavy-equipment battery system, powering gear such as concrete cut-off saws, breakers and light towers.
          </p>

          <p>
            M18 tools come in tiers. Brushed models form the entry level, compact brushless models fill the middle, and <strong>FUEL</strong> tools sit on top with Milwaukee's strongest brushless motors and most advanced electronics. Plenty of trade buyers skip the lower tiers and buy FUEL for drills, impacts and saws from the start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How DeWalt splits 20V MAX, ATOMIC, XR and FLEXVOLT</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>20V MAX</strong> is DeWalt's main platform. Its tools are grouped as standard 20V MAX, the compact <strong>ATOMIC</strong> range and the premium brushless <strong>XR</strong> range. <strong>FLEXVOLT</strong> batteries change between 20 and 60 volts to suit the tool they are in, so one pack can power DeWalt's biggest cordless saws, grinders and outdoor tools and still slide onto any 20V MAX tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which compact 12V line is deeper?</h3>

          <p>
            Milwaukee's. DeWalt also sells a <strong>12V MAX XTREME</strong> subcompact line, and it is a good, expanding range, but it does not yet reach the breadth of M12. That depth is one of the clearest wins Milwaukee has. If a big compact lineup for cramped spaces matters to you, it pushes the decision toward red.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">VOLTAGE MYTH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">20V MAX and M18 belong to the same voltage class.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">DeWalt quotes maximum initial voltage; Milwaukee quotes nominal voltage. Underneath, both are five-cell 18-volt lithium systems. Judge a tool by its torque, speed and motor tier, never by the number on its battery.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Brand Has Better Batteries?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither wins outright on everyday packs, but DeWalt has the bigger structural difference in FLEXVOLT. Both brands offer compact packs of about 2.0 amp-hours for light tools and larger packs from roughly 5.0 up to 12.0 amp-hours for saws, grinders and outdoor gear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What decides runtime and power?</h3>

          <p>
            Capacity sets how long a pack runs. How much current it can push, which is what high-draw tools care about, comes down to the cell technology and how many cells are wired in parallel. That is why two packs with the same amp-hour label can feel different in a heavy saw.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HIGH OUTPUT and FORGE vs POWERSTACK</h3>

          <p>
            For demanding tools, Milwaukee sells <strong>HIGH OUTPUT</strong> RedLithium packs and the newer <strong>FORGE</strong> packs, both built on higher-performance cells. DeWalt's answer is <strong>POWERSTACK</strong>, which swaps cylindrical cells for stacked pouch cells to get strong output from a smaller, lighter pack. Owners of both brands commonly report a noticeable gain from these premium packs, both in heavy cutting and in compact drills and impacts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">FLEXVOLT vs Milwaukee's high-output approach</h3>

          <p>
            With FLEXVOLT, a single battery runs both 20V MAX and 60V MAX tools. A framer can drive a big worm-drive style circular saw and a drill off the same pack. Milwaukee reaches similar territory with high-output M18 packs paired with FUEL tools, and hands the very heaviest equipment to its separate MX FUEL system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long are the batteries covered?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually for less time than the tools. Milwaukee commonly covers many RedLithium packs for three years, and DeWalt includes its packs in its three-year limited warranty, though individual packs and promotions differ, so read the current terms at purchase. Adapters that let one brand's battery run the other brand's tools are sold by third parties, but neither maker supports them and they can affect warranty coverage.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Battery Basics</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Nominal voltage of both M18 and 20V MAX</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-12 Ah</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical capacity range across both lineups</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">FLEXVOLT high-voltage mode for big DeWalt tools</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Packs that fit across brands without an adapter</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="core-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Makes the Better Drill, Impact Driver and Saw?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On drills and impact drivers it is a draw; on saws DeWalt has the longer pedigree. Both brands' flagship drivers rank among the best cordless tools sold, and the gap between them is small enough that feel and features should decide it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drills and impact drivers</h3>

          <p>
            Milwaukee's M18 FUEL drill and impact driver and DeWalt's 20V MAX XR counterparts swap the lead each time a new generation arrives. Published torque numbers keep leapfrogging, and between current flagships the differences are slight enough that ergonomics and features count for more than peak output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle both in a store if you get the chance. DeWalt drills tend to feel slightly lighter at the nose, and many users like how they grip. Milwaukee's FUEL impacts are known for drive control modes that cap speed or torque so small screws do not snap, and DeWalt's XR impacts offer comparable precision modes. Most models from both brands include an LED light, a belt clip and a bit holder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Circular, miter and table saws</h3>

          <p>
            DeWalt's FLEXVOLT circular, miter and table saws stand out for anyone who wants cordless saws that cut like corded ones. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL circular saws, along with its miter and table saws, compete hard. DeWalt, though, has the longer track record in jobsite table and miter saws, and that history keeps many finish carpenters loyal to yellow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Impact wrenches for automotive work</h3>

          <p>
            This race is tighter than many expect. Mechanics favor Milwaukee's M18 FUEL high-torque impact wrenches, and DeWalt's 20V MAX XR high-torque models are close rivals. Either will spin off lug nuts with ease, but always do the final tightening on wheels by hand with a calibrated wrench from our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">best torque wrench guide</a>, because an impact can blow past the specified torque in a second.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the hand tools that sit beside either platform, a budget mechanic's set is often a better buy than branded sockets. Our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">Tekton tools review</a> looks at one of the strongest values in sockets and wrenches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specialty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Brand Has More Specialty and Outdoor Tools?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee, for most trades, while DeWalt leads in carpentry and general construction specialties. Outdoors, the two are roughly even and neither is the category leader.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For plumbers, electricians and mechanics</h3>

          <p>
            This is Milwaukee's strongest ground. Across M18 and M12 you will find copper and PEX press tools, pipe threaders, drain cleaners, band saws, cable crimpers and cutters, knockout tools, grease guns and dozens of automotive tools. Plumbers and electricians often go red simply because no other brand puts so many of their everyday tools on one battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For carpenters and builders</h3>

          <p>
            DeWalt's specialty depth lies in carpentry and general construction: nailers in several gauges, routers, planers, track saws, concrete tools and a wide choice of jobsite saws. Builders rate its 20V MAX framing and finish nailers highly, and FLEXVOLT runs heavy tools such as large grinders and core drills.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoor power equipment</h3>

          <p>
            Each brand sells string trimmers, blowers, chainsaws, hedge trimmers and mowers. Milwaukee aims its M18 FUEL outdoor range, including the QUIK-LOK attachment system, at landscapers. DeWalt's 20V and 60V outdoor tools are a solid fit for homeowners. Still, neither is the best outdoor brand overall; dedicated platforms such as EGO lead in mowers and blowers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lights, storage and laser levels</h3>

          <p>
            Both brands cover lighting and accessories well. Milwaukee's lights, radios and heated jackets have a big following, and DeWalt's range is broad too. Each also sells modular storage, PACKOUT from Milwaukee and ToughSystem from DeWalt, and because boxes and organizers only lock into their own system, storage becomes one more reason owners stay with one color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measuring tools deserve a mention as well. DeWalt's green laser levels run on its 12V and 20V batteries, which is handy on site; our <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser level guide</a> shows how they stack up against other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Has the Longer Warranty?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee does: most M18 and M12 power tools carry a five-year limited warranty, against DeWalt's standard three years. DeWalt narrows the gap with a free first year of service and a money back guarantee.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Milwaukee covers</h3>

          <p>
            The five-year term applies to most M18 and M12 tools, but some categories, including certain outdoor tools, MX FUEL equipment and instruments, come with different terms, and batteries usually have separate, shorter coverage. Registering the tool and holding on to the receipt makes any claim easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What DeWalt covers</h3>

          <p>
            Most DeWalt power tools get a three-year limited warranty, one year of free service for routine maintenance and, on many products bought new, a 90-day money back guarantee. The free first-year service and the satisfaction guarantee suit buyers who want to try a tool and send it back if it does not work out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How good is the service network?</h3>

          <p>
            Each brand operates authorized service centers and offers factory service for mail-in repairs, with turnaround that depends on region and season. Tradespeople commonly report that having a service center nearby counts as much as the written warranty, because a tool stuck in a shipping box for weeks costs them money.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the warranty gap matter to a homeowner?</h3>

          <p>
            Less than the numbers suggest. Light household use seldom wears out a premium drill inside five years. For contractors running tools every day, the extra coverage is a genuine advantage, and it is part of why Milwaukee has gained ground in the commercial trades.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Milwaukee or DeWalt Cheaper?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At list price, neither: comparable tiers cost about the same. At the time of writing, a premium brushless drill and impact driver kit with two batteries and a charger typically sells for roughly $250 to $400 from either brand, and bare tools for roughly $100 to $250. Entry-level kits from both come in lower, and flagship FUEL or XR tools sit at the top of that range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How promotions change the price</h3>

          <p>
            Deals shift the math more than list prices do. DeWalt kits often show up with bonus batteries or free tools at many retailers, and holiday sales can bring steep markdowns. Milwaukee runs frequent battery-and-tool bundles, particularly at Home Depot, where a starter battery kit can come with a free bare tool. With either brand, a patient shopper can save a large share of list price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When are the best sales?</h3>

          <p>
            Holiday periods, Father's Day and spring tool events tend to bring the lowest prices on both brands. If you are building a kit over time, it pays to time your battery and starter kit purchases for those windows, since bundles at Home Depot and bonus-battery DeWalt kits are where the extra value tends to sit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Where Can You Buy Milwaukee and DeWalt Tools?
          </h2>

          <p>
            DeWalt is far easier to find. It is stocked at Home Depot, Lowe's, Amazon, Walmart, Ace and a long list of independent dealers. In the United States, Milwaukee sells mainly through Home Depot, plus professional distributors and specialty dealers. If Lowe's is your store, that alone may settle the question in DeWalt's favor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you avoid fake or gray-market tools?</h3>

          <p>
            Buy from authorized retailers or the brand's listed dealers. Counterfeit and gray-market tools and batteries circulate for both brands and may come without warranty coverage. Batteries are the biggest concern, since a counterfeit pack carries a real safety risk, so be especially careful where you buy them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Should You Buy Milwaukee or DeWalt for Your Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Trades that need specialty tools should buy Milwaukee, builders lean DeWalt, and homeowners should buy whichever platform they already own or can shop for most easily. Here is how that breaks down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plumbers, electricians and HVAC technicians</h3>

          <p>
            Milwaukee is the better fit. Nothing matches the range of trade-specific tools on M18 and M12, the compact M12 line excels at tight work, and the five-year warranty suits tools that run every day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Framers, carpenters and remodelers</h3>

          <p>
            Either brand works, but DeWalt holds an edge thanks to FLEXVOLT saws, a deep lineup of nailers and jobsite saws, and wide retail availability. Those are the reasons many general contractors run yellow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mechanics and automotive enthusiasts</h3>

          <p>
            Most lean Milwaukee for its M12 ratchets, compact impacts and FUEL high-torque wrenches. DeWalt's automotive range keeps expanding, but Milwaukee has led this space for years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Homeowners</h3>

          <p>
            Choose by where you shop, which deals are running and how the drill feels in your hand; both will handle years of household projects. If you already own a few batteries from one brand, that brand is your answer. Someone who needs a drill, an impact driver, a circular saw and a handful of other tools over ten years will be happy with either.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only need a drill and a few basics for the occasional job, check budget platforms before paying professional prices. Our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a> explains where that platform holds up and where it falls short.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why one platform beats two</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, commit to it. In cordless tools the real spending goes into batteries and chargers, and running two brands doubles that spend while making each pack you own half as flexible.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your second tool picks your brand.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Before you buy anything, write down the next five tools you expect to need. Choose the platform that sells every one of them in the tier you want. That list will steer you better than whichever drill tops this year's spec sheet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes With Cordless Tool Platforms
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating 20V and 18V as different power classes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                DeWalt's 20V MAX and Milwaukee's M18 share the same 18-volt nominal rating; the 20V label is the maximum initial voltage with no load. Buyers who judge power by the printed number reach the wrong conclusion. Compare published torque, speed and motor tier instead, and hold both tools before you decide.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a drill before choosing a platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Your first tool locks in the battery you will keep buying for years. Before checkout, write down the next several tools you expect to need and confirm the platform sells each one. A decision made on a single drill deal often ends with paying for a second set of batteries later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Splitting brands to chase deals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bargain bare tool from the other brand stops being a bargain once it needs its own batteries and charger. Running two platforms doubles battery spending and makes every pack you own less useful. Unless a tool genuinely exists on only one platform, stay with the batteries already on your shelf.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on battery adapters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Third-party adapters that let one brand's packs run the other's tools have no support from either maker. They can bypass protections designed into the original battery and tool pairing, may affect warranty coverage and add bulk. They might be tolerable for light, occasional use, but never as a platform strategy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from unauthorized sellers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gray-market and counterfeit tools and batteries circulate for both brands. They may carry no warranty, and a fake battery can be a genuine fire risk. Stick to authorized retailers and listed dealers, inspect the packaging closely, and treat a price far below the usual range as a warning sign, not a find.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a Milwaukee battery in a DeWalt tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not without a workaround. The two brands use different battery shapes and terminals, and neither company sells an adapter for the other's packs. Third-party adapters are on the market, but neither maker supports them and they can put your warranty at risk. The sensible plan is to commit to one platform and grow your kit around its batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee or DeWalt more durable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over the long haul the two are close, because both brands build their professional tiers for daily jobsite use. On paper, Milwaukee backs most M18 and M12 tools for five years, while DeWalt gives three years plus one year of free service. Owner feedback is strong for both. How you treat the tool and its batteries, and which tier you buy, matter more than red or yellow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the same company make Milwaukee and Ryobi?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Both belong to Techtronic Industries, a Hong Kong based group that also owns other tool and floor care brands. Milwaukee is its professional line and Ryobi its consumer line; they are engineered separately and run on different batteries. A Ryobi ONE+ pack will not fit a Milwaukee M18 tool, and an M18 pack will not fit a Ryobi tool either.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is FLEXVOLT worth it for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, no. A FLEXVOLT pack is bigger, heavier and pricier than a standard 20V MAX pack, and its advantage only shows on DeWalt's 60V saws, grinders and outdoor tools. Drills, impact drivers and light saws run perfectly well on ordinary 20V MAX packs. If you buy a 60V tool later, you can add a FLEXVOLT pack at that point.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand has better deals?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither has a lasting edge, because both run promotions all year. DeWalt kits often include bonus batteries or a free tool, and more retailers put them on sale. Milwaukee leans on battery-and-tool bundles, especially at Home Depot. The best prices for both tend to land around the holidays, Father's Day and spring tool events, so a patient buyer seldom pays full list price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is M12 or 12V MAX XTREME better for compact tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                M12 is the stronger compact platform today. Its lineup is broader, with many plumbing, electrical and automotive specialty tools that DeWalt's 12V line has not matched yet. DeWalt's 12V MAX XTREME is well made and still growing, so it handles basic compact work fine. For tight spaces and trade-specific jobs, though, Milwaukee M12 has the clear edge right now.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are Milwaukee and DeWalt tools made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both brands build tools in several countries, including China, Mexico, Vietnam and the United States, and both assemble some products domestically from global parts. Where a given tool comes from depends on the model and can shift over time. If origin matters to you, read the packaging or product listing for that exact tool instead of assuming by brand.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

